WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.010
Ali, I'm so excited that you're here.

2
00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:07.080
You're so darn funny and amazing.

3
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.510
Would you like to hit some headlines with us?

4
00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:10.760
Let's hit them, baby.

5
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:11.810
All right, Ross, kick it off.

6
00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.660
All right, Ali, we are going to kick it off

7
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.630
with the latest on Jeopardy!.

8
00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.100
USA Today reports the show's announced a lineup

9
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.300
of celeb guest hosts who are going to just do it

10
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.950
for a little bit, just for funsies.

11
00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:24.380
They include Anderson Cooper, Savannah Guthrie

12
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.254
and our friend Dr. Oz.

13
00:00:26.254 --> 00:00:27.087 line:15%
I know.

14
00:00:27.087 --> 00:00:29.940 line:15%
And I remember, Ali, and I wanted to ask you specifically.

15
00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.810 line:15%
Howard Stern was talking to your husband,

16
00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.420 line:15%
George Stephanopoulos, and he actually said

17
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.440 line:15%
that George would make a good host for Jeopardy!.

18
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.250 line:15%
So, can you break any news here?

19
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.053 line:15%
Has George been offered a gig?

20
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.868
All I can tell you is that George is not that smart.

21
00:00:45.868 --> 00:00:48.450
(laughing)

22
00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:51.610
So if he did host Jeopardy!, he'd need an ear piece

23
00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:55.897
and I'd be having to feed him, which gets complicated.

24
00:00:55.897 --> 00:01:00.020
I think George is extremely busy with GMA

25
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.580
and his Sunday show.

26
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.890
And of course all my sexual needs.

27
00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.520
So I don't think he'd be able to fit

28
00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:07.990
anything else in his schedule.

29
00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.650
I mean, maybe Grey's Anatomy once in a while,

30
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.160
but he's a busy guy.

31
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.770
I think you could do it too though, Ali,

32
00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.210
you'd be great.
Oh, I would love that.

33
00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.940
I've seen you do, I mean, I just love watching you.

34
00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.450
I am all ear and all eyes.
How fun would that be?

35
00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:23.283
Oh my gosh.

36
00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:24.840
Maybe we should start doing segments

37
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.150
on The Drew Show where we do our own Jeopardy!.

38
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:32.130
Why do we have to wait for ABC to come to us?

39
00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.480
I love answering trivia questions

40
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.650
and I love you and we love games, so, okay.

41
00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:39.570
It's a no brainer.

42
00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.583
All right, we'll offline as soon as this segment is over.

43
00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.960
I, by the way, I love how your husband

44
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.660
just gets you and loves your humor and celebrates you.

45
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:50.720
It's such a good--

46
00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.370
We couldn't be married if that was not the dynamic,

47
00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:57.100
because people were so, and they still are,

48
00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:59.210
so curious about us.

49
00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:02.260
And I always say, "you don't want two of me."

50
00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:06.000
If I'd married Carrot Top, it would be a nightmare.

51
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.970
And you know, if George married some astro scientist

52
00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:11.270
with no personality,

53
00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.170
it'd be a little bland at a dinner party.

54
00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.570
So I think it's, I think it's a good mix, you know?

55
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.750
And now we have two daughters that have

56
00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:18.730
kind of a little bit of both.

57
00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:21.350
I always say they have his brain and my legs,

58
00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.457
and if it were the other way around, they'd be screwed.

59
00:02:23.457 --> 00:02:25.360
(laughing)

60
00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:26.780
Maybe that's what you need, Drew,

61
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.810
is like the counterbalance to you.

62
00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:29.643
You know what I mean?

63
00:02:29.643 --> 00:02:32.227
I know like Joe Manganiello was like,

64
00:02:32.227 --> 00:02:33.850
"You need someone who can one up you."

65
00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:36.004
I was like, "I think the Earth might implode."

66
00:02:36.004 --> 00:02:37.290
(laughing)

67
00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.740
Maybe someone to like mellow you out.

68
00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:40.910
Not that you're you, but you know what I mean?

69
00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:41.743
Like the opposite of--

70
00:02:41.743 --> 00:02:44.197
Valium in the form of a man. Perfect.

71
00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:45.898
(laughing)

72
00:02:45.898 --> 00:02:47.570
That would be an emotional support dog.

73
00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:48.403
Go ahead.

74
00:02:48.403 --> 00:02:50.103
I have one of those, Douglas.

75
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.520
I have a trivia question.

76
00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.743
How often should you replace your hairbrush?

77
00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.460
I know that I'm gonna say the wrong thing,

78
00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.310
because I think I still have my hairbrush

79
00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.677
from freshman year of college.

80
00:03:03.677 --> 00:03:04.510
(laughing)

81
00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.850
Real Simple asked an expert and they say

82
00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:08.990
that we should be replacing our brushes

83
00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:11.840
every six months to a year.

84
00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.680
And, you should be deep cleaning the barnacles

85
00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:17.790 line:15%
off that bad boy once a month.

86
00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.720 line:15%
Barnacles? Barnacles?

87
00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.517 line:15%
I know, sloughing, like a whale.

88
00:03:22.517 --> 00:03:26.110 line:15%
I've seen my girlfriends getting ready before we go out.

89
00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:27.047 line:15%
And I always say like,

90
00:03:27.047 --> 00:03:28.920 line:15%
"I would never judge you, but you're disgusting."

91
00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.930 line:15%
Because some of them look like a true rat's nest.

92
00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.383 line:15%
Ali, what's the state of your brush?

93
00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:38.540 line:15%
Well, I think the state of my brush is it's a chia pet,

94
00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.878
kind of, I haven't found any barnacles.

95
00:03:40.878 --> 00:03:44.780
I see live animals, but it's hard to tell

96
00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.100
'cause my daughters take my brush too.

97
00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.040
So it's a little bit of everything.

98
00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.020
Ali, quickly tell us about your podcast,

99
00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.540
Go Ask Ali, and what we can expect this season.

100
00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.040
This past season was all about

101
00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.040
how to grow a teenager in a pandemic.

102
00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:02.280
This season is all about how to grow a relationship

103
00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.490 line:15%
in a pandemic, in a crazy world.

104
00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:09.310 line:15%
And I mean, growing a relationship with a sibling,

105
00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:12.310
with a spouse, with a lover, with an aging parent,

106
00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:15.420
with the UPS guy, and I've actually found

107
00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:18.620
that I'm doing an episode about growing a relationship

108
00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:21.540
with yourself, because during the pandemic,

109
00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.147
every night with my two daughters, I would say,

110
00:04:23.147 --> 00:04:24.930
"Let's have an ice cream bar."

111
00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.320
And I went to the doctor and my cholesterol

112
00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:31.460
was like 310, which is, I shouldn't be alive.

113
00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:34.870
And I realized I'm just not taking care of myself at all

114
00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:39.190
because I'm Zooming, and so you can't get the whole picture.

115
00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:40.980
And I just haven't been healthy at all.

116
00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.770
So I stopped the ice cream for a while

117
00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.810
and I started walking with my emotional support dog, Cooper,

118
00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.340
who's my other husband, and sort of started to get healthy,

119
00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.780
but I realized so many, don't you guys find

120
00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.760
that so many relationships during this time

121
00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:57.470
have been fractured or you're not seeing your girlfriends

122
00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:59.910
as much and maybe you don't need all these people

123
00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.210
in your life, maybe you just need a core group?

124
00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:05.180
So I've had everybody, Glennon Doyle, everybody,

125
00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.400
my bestie Mariska Hargitay,

126
00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.120
like so many people weighing in

127
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.150
on how we grow these healthy relationships right now.

128
00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.550
I love that you're talking about the relationship

129
00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:15.670
with yourself, too.

130
00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:16.520
And reevaluating.

131
00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:19.470
I think this pandemic has made us reevaluate everything.

132
00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:21.950
Listen, everyone is going to be in relationships

133
00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:22.950
throughout their life.

134
00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.240
I love that you're covering all different types of them

135
00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.490
because there are all different types out there,

136
00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:30.080
but we are always navigating relationships.

137
00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:32.580
And everything you just said was so astute.

138
00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.090
I'm just gonna hang on your every word.

139
00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:37.960
Ali Wentworth, her podcast, Go Ask Ali,

140
00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:40.780
is out now with new episodes coming in March

141
00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:42.190
and I can't thank you enough.

142
00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:43.330
And let's sidebar

143
00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:45.344
about more, more, more.
I love you both so much.

144
00:05:45.344 --> 00:05:47.927 line:15%
(upbeat music)

